fix(arithmetic): premultiply and unpremultiply on float carriers (closes #631)

`try_premultiply` and `try_unpremultiply` fell into `depth_max`'s "the
arithmetic operations do not support float rasters yet" panic when they were
handed a float raster, from inside the fallible form, which is the one thing a
`try_` method is not allowed to do. It was always reachable, but OpenEXR (#626)
and FITS (#625) hand back float pixel data straight out of an untrusted file,
so a caller who loads an EXR and calls a premultiply helper now gets there on
ordinary input.

I implemented the float carriers rather than refusing them. Nothing was left to
guess at: libvips defines both ops on FLOAT and this build runs them,
`unpremultiply_factor` already carried the dead zone and the alpha clip from
#611, and the resize path has been doing the same arithmetic internally since
#604.

Two things I measured rather than transcribed. `max_alpha` comes from the
raster's `Interpretation` and not the sample depth, the way
`vips_interpretation_max_alpha` supplies it, so an scRGB raster (what an RGB
OpenEXR load is tagged) divides by 1.0 where an untagged one divides by 255 and
an Rgb16-tagged one by 65535. And the float arm runs in `f32`, not `f64`: the C
macros land the multiplier in a `float` before the colour multiply, so the
result rounds twice, and the single-rounded f64 form misses vips by about an
ulp on ordinary values. The two-rounding model is bit-exact against the binary
on all 16384 samples of a 64x64 four-band sweep, for both max_alpha 255 and
1.0.

The unsigned carriers are untouched: still f64, still rounded and saturated
into the input depth, still against `depth_max`. Routing them through the
interpretation as well would have moved an untagged Multi16 raster from 65535
to 255, and that is a separate question.
